Residual Network.
Based on the above plain network, we insert shortcut connections (Fig. 3, right) which turn the network into its counterpart residual version.
The identity shortcuts (Eqn.(1)) can be directly used when the input and output are of the same dimensions (solid line shortcuts in Fig. 3).
When the dimensions increase (dotted line shortcuts in Fig. 3), we consider two options:
(A) The shortcut still performs identity mapping, with extra zero entries padded for increasing dimensions. This option introduces no extra parameter;
(B) The projection shortcut in Eqn.(2) is used to match dimensions (done by 1 \\times 1 convolutions).
For both options, when the shortcuts go across feature maps of two sizes, they are performed with a stride of 2.', '4', '4', NULL, '2026-08-11 17:04:18.922886+00'),
